    "MEDICATIONS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE",
          "canonical_heading": "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Activated charcoal (1–2 g/kg) within 2 hours post exposure; repeat activated charcoal for ER formulations.",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Atropine (0.02–0.04 mg/kg IV) for sinus bradycardia.",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Intravenous fat emulsion (IFE)—may bind lipid-soluble drugs, increase Ca available for muscle contraction, and provide energy source for heart muscle. IFE 20% dose—1.5 mL/kg IV bolus over 1 min followed by CRI of 0.25 mL/kg/min for 30–60 min. Additional IV doses—1.5 ml/kg every 4–6 hours or CRI of 0.5 mL/kg/h; monitor serum for lipemia every 2–4 hours to determine if additional dosing needed.",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "High-dose insulin euglycemia (HIE) treatment—may provide energy for heart muscle and increase Ca available for muscle contraction. Recommended treatment:",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Check blood glucose (BG) concentration",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "and administer dextrose if BG<100 mg/dL.",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Administer regular insulin at 1 unit/kg IV",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "followed by CRI at 0.5–2 units/kg/h. Increase every 10 min as needed up to maximum dose of 10 units/kg/h. When clinical signs resolve, decrease insulin by 1–2 units/kg/h.",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Monitor BG every 10 min while titrating",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "insulin dosing. Once insulin dosing is stabilized, check BG every 30–60 min. Dextrose supplementation IV will be needed to maintain normal BG concentrations. Central line placement recommended for frequent glucose monitoring and dextrose administration during HIE treatment. May need to continue dextrose supplementation up to 24 hours after discontinuation of insulin.",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Monitor potassium concentration every hour",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "during titration, then every 6 hours. Administer",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "potassium chloride to keep potassium concentrations within normal range.",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Calcium gluconate 10% 0.5–1.5 ml/kg IV over 10–20 min, followed by CRI 0.1– 0.15 ml/kg/h. May increase calcium available for heart muscle contraction.",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Glucagon—0.05–0.2 mg/kg slow IV bolus followed by CRI of 0.1–0.15 mg/kg/h. May not be as effective as IFE or HIE therapy.",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Sympathomimetics or vasopressin should only be used in cases refractory to other treatments.",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "CONTRAINDICATION/POSSIBLE",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "INTERACTIONS",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Vasopressors/sympathomimetics may not be",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 3,
              "subheading": "INTERACTIONS"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "as effective as other therapy and may make",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 3,
              "subheading": "INTERACTIONS"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "patients less responsive to treatment.",
              "pdf_page": 253,
              "printed_page": 171,
              "column": 3,
              "subheading": "INTERACTIONS"
            }
          ]
        },
